WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:08.560
Kilian: How do you see yourself in the future, in running?

00:00:08.560 --> 00:00:21.760
Hans: I'm a huge fan of both you and Jim Walmsley. You two are my top athletes in the sport, so of course I'm trying to do a lot of the things you both did.

00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:37.200
Kilian: It's very cool to talk with you while you are still so young, almost like you are just starting. I remember being your age. How old are you now?

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:50.640
Hans: I'm 26.

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:50.640
Kilian: I remember that age being so fun. You are discovering races, discovering your capacities, and finding out what you are able to do. That excitement is something pretty unique.

00:00:50.640 --> 00:01:21.680
Hans: It's a cool place to be. I was talking with Hayden Hawks two weeks ago, and we talked about the "Kilian effect." For the runners five years younger than you, there is this huge generation of trail runners who were inspired by you to start trail running. Obviously, that brought in some really talented people.

00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:32.320
Hans: I feel like there has almost been a skip in generations. You don't see many 25-to-30-year-olds, so I feel like I'm in a unique spot, almost alone in my age group, which is interesting.

00:01:32.320 --> 00:02:11.680
Kilian: Yes. You still have that youthful excitement, but at the same time you already have experience. You have been running for a few years, you know how to race, and you know how to execute. That makes it the perfect moment.

00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:20.160
Kilian: What you have done over the last two years, breaking records and pushing limits, has been exciting to follow. It is cool to see how your generation is pushing the limits so much further.

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:54.320
Kilian: You are not afraid to look at what came before and think, "I can go faster." Jim Walmsley ran incredibly fast, and you think you can run faster. Jim probably saw Scott Jurek and thought the same thing. That is how the sport evolves, each generation looks at the one before and believes it can improve.

00:02:54.320 --> 00:03:37.680
Hans: Yes, it's about taking notes, learning, and trying to improve. I also think the sport is professionalizing. In the past, people had a trail-running season, then a ski season, and so on. I grew up in Georgia, so there was no ski season. I just trained year-round.

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:42.880
Hans: With things like high-carb fueling and all the new knowledge, I listen to podcasts, read books, take notes, and try to be a student of the sport. I am willing to sacrifice a lot to do everything I can to get faster. I think it's a really cool time in the sport, and over the next 10 years we will probably see a lot of change.

00:03:42.880 --> 00:04:15.760
Kilian: Yes. As you said, people are becoming more professional. Not only the athletes, but also the structures around them. Before, athletes might have been professional, but they were often alone. Now there are coaches, nutritionists, and many more people bringing knowledge into the sport.

00:04:15.760 --> 00:04:22.720
Kilian: How did you end up doing Western States, coming from Georgia?

00:04:22.720 --> 00:05:27.920
Hans: In college, when I was running, I started reading trail-running books. I read one of your books, Scott Jurek's books, Dean Karnazes, David Goggins, and others. In Scott Jurek's Eat and Run, he talked about Western States over and over again, and that intrigued me.

00:04:43.520 --> 00:05:09.680
Hans: Then we started putting on the livestream for some of those years, so I started watching the race. At first, I was oblivious to how hard trail running actually is. I remember seeing the paces and thinking, "I could run that pace, that should be easy."

00:04:57.840 --> 00:05:21.040
Hans: Then I was surprised when I started running trail races and realized, no, these are very fast times. I quickly became fascinated by it. The prestige of Western States itself, and the fact that everyone who has won it is legendary, makes it really exciting for me to compete there.

00:05:27.920 --> 00:05:52.400
Kilian: It is fun to see how people start trail running. You came from a track background, running in school, while other people come from mountain sports. But in the end we are all doing the same thing, running on trails.

00:05:52.400 --> 00:06:32.800
Hans: I want to read more of your books, but I read Run or Die. In the early chapters you talked about the value you found in endurance and athletic performance. I remember relating to that so much.

00:06:03.280 --> 00:06:22.160
Hans: When I first got into trail running, I had no intention of going pro. I did not think I was capable of that. All I knew was that I thought it was so cool to push myself to my limits and explore mountains. That was entirely intrinsic motivation.

00:06:22.160 --> 00:06:37.600
Hans: I found so much value in that, and I want to thank you for it. You showed me that other people felt the same way, and I really appreciated that.

00:06:37.600 --> 00:07:00.720
Kilian: Thanks, man. It is nice to inspire athletes like you to push things forward. You moved to Colorado so you could train more for this, and you are really committed.

00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:08.160
Kilian: What does your training ecosystem look like? Do you have a coach? A nutritionist? A strength coach?

00:07:08.160 --> 00:07:52.400
Hans: My coach is David Roche. I do not have a nutritionist, and my diet could definitely be better.

00:07:19.680 --> 00:07:31.200
Kilian: Then you have something to improve. That is good. Knowing that there are things you can improve is always useful.

00:07:31.200 --> 00:07:44.400
Hans: Absolutely. I focus more on calories than anything else, so I eat a lot of frozen pizza, burgers, and things like that. I know it could be better, but I am slowly adapting as I go.

00:07:44.400 --> 00:08:14.320
Hans: David helps me a lot. I did my undergraduate degree in kinesiology, then went back and got my master's in kinesiology. I like exercise science, so I enjoy having a bit of a say in things like strength training. David and I talk about training in different ways, and it is fun for me to experiment.

00:08:14.320 --> 00:08:37.600
Kilian: You have to find your own way and discover what works for you. The principle of individuality is something we often forget. We see someone doing something and think we should do the same, but we all adapt differently to different types of stimuli.

00:08:37.600 --> 00:08:51.920
Hans: Definitely. With the age of media we live in now, you could almost say media can act as a coach. Listening to podcasts and reading books gives you access to so much information. It is a huge resource today.

00:08:51.920 --> 00:09:26.400
Kilian: Yes, but the challenge is knowing which parts of that information are relevant and which are not. We have access to so much information, but it is difficult to separate what is truly useful from what is not.

00:09:26.400 --> 00:09:53.840
Kilian: It is the same with other athletes. Talented people can do very stupid things and still have good results. You might see someone doing something and think, "I should do the same," but it may not be the right thing for you. Finding the right way takes time and experience.

00:09:53.840 --> 00:10:22.560
Hans: Yes. Western States is in three days. I am from the United States, so that race really fits me. But I am also fascinated by UTMB, so I am racing CCC this year.

00:10:12.640 --> 00:10:17.360
Kilian: I think that race will suit you well.

00:10:17.360 --> 00:10:45.680
Hans: I think so too. It will be a good introduction to European racing. I have never been to Europe, so it should be a great introduction. But at least in my competitive career, I think Western States and UTMB will be my two target races.

00:10:30.240 --> 00:11:07.200
Hans: We will see where that goes. Right now I am obsessed with the competitive side of the sport, but I also love adventures. Things like States of Elevation are so cool to me.

00:10:45.680 --> 00:11:01.360
Hans: It was really cool that I got to come out for that. It was so windy and so cold. People asked what we talked about, and I told them that half the time we could barely hear each other because of the wind. But it was a really cool experience.

00:11:01.360 --> 00:11:12.720
Hans: Maybe long adventures like that could be something for me later on. But right now I am pretty obsessed with Western States and UTMB.

00:11:12.720 --> 00:11:31.280
Kilian: Those are huge goals, but you definitely have all the ingredients to succeed. As you said, you are a student of the sport. Understanding your body and how you function is very important if you want to win these races.

00:11:31.280 --> 00:11:36.960
Hans: Do you have any advice for me, as someone early in my career trying to tackle these big goals?

00:11:36.960 --> 00:12:34.240
Kilian: I think you already have this openness to learning, and to learning about your body. That is very important. In long-distance racing, it is a lot about resilience. At our level, almost everyone can run the first six, seven, or eight hours at the pace we run.

00:12:02.080 --> 00:12:26.560
Kilian: But what happens from there to the finish line is what matters. In Western States, it might be the last five hours. In UTMB, it might be the last 10 hours. The question is who is more resilient metabolically, muscularly, and in every other way.

00:12:26.560 --> 00:13:05.840
Kilian: That is difficult to figure out, and it requires experience. Your race here last year was very valuable because it probably helped you understand the race better. Small things you do can change the outcome. In a short race, they may not matter much, but in a long race they do.

00:13:05.840 --> 00:13:31.680
Kilian: A lot of it is about listening to your body. You need to understand your fueling, your muscles, and what you are feeling. Can you eat a bit more? Do you need to eat a bit less? It is good to have a plan, but you also need to adapt while racing. You might say, "That was the plan, but now I feel I need to adjust it." That is very important in races like this.

00:13:31.680 --> 00:14:02.320
Hans: Absolutely. I think you said something in one of your videos last year. You said you were chasing physiological changes over decades. I loved that. I thought that was so powerful because so many people are chasing quick fitness.

00:13:50.080 --> 00:14:02.320
Hans: Real fitness comes from months and years of changing the body. I have used that quote all year when talking to people.

00:14:02.320 --> 00:14:47.200
Kilian: We all want the easy solution. Take this pill and you will get stronger. Do this interval session and you will get stronger. But reality is not like that. When you look at successful athletes, they have been doing this for decades.

00:14:20.240 --> 00:14:39.280
Kilian: Even successful young athletes have usually been practicing sport or physical activity for many years. Being able to stay healthy and train week after week, day after day, month after month, and year after year changes your biology. That is what makes you the athlete you are today.

00:14:39.280 --> 00:15:06.320
Kilian: It is rare for young athletes to have that mindset. Many want to perform immediately. They say, "I want to train for four months and perform. What should I do?" The answer is to be patient. Things arrive over time. I think you have that mindset, and that will make you great.

00:15:06.320 --> 00:15:26.880
Hans: That is good to hear. David and I talk about that a lot. I hope my competitive career will last another 12 to 14 years if everything goes according to plan. The question is how to train hard, stay fit, avoid injury, avoid overtraining, and be patient.

00:15:26.880 --> 00:15:44.560
Kilian: It is better to be able to train every day for years than to do one crazy session and then be injured. I wish you the best of luck.

00:15:44.560 --> 00:15:54.400
Hans: Thanks. Hopefully we can admire the terrain together again, like last year.

00:15:54.400 --> 00:16:11.440
Hans: One of my memories from last year was when we were in the high country. I think you were right behind me, and I kept looking left. You said, "It's beautiful, isn't it?"

00:16:05.040 --> 00:16:11.440
Kilian: It is beautiful. We cannot take for granted that we are able to do this. It is amazing.

00:16:11.440 --> 00:16:24.960
Hans: Absolutely. Thanks for having me on. This is really cool. And thanks for everything you have done for the sport. Like I said, I am a huge fan, and I am honored to be racing you again on Saturday.

00:16:19.840 --> 00:16:24.960
Kilian: Same to you. I wish you the best of luck.

00:16:24.960 --> 00:16:27.093
Hans: Perfect. This is exciting.

